remount-secure drop-in configuration
=====================================

remount-secure reads '*.conf' fragments from, in increasing precedence:

  /etc/remount-secure.d/*.conf           (admin)
  /usr/local/etc/remount-secure.d/*.conf (local admin, highest precedence)

Fragments are sourced as shell 'key=value'; later files win. Only '*.conf'
files are read (this README is ignored).

Supported key:

  remount_secure_level=3

Levels:

  0  off (remount-secure no-ops)
  1  nosuid, nodev
  2  + noexec for most mount points, except /home
  3  + noexec including /home (default)

Example - lower to level 2 so executables in /home keep working:

  printf '%s\n' 'remount_secure_level=2' | sudo tee /etc/remount-secure.d/50_local.conf

Or use the helper (writes /etc/remount-secure.d/40_managed.conf):

  sudo remount-secure --set-level 2

A reboot applies the change (or run 'sudo remount-secure --force <level>').

Customizing per-mountpoint options
----------------------------------

A fragment may also redefine a mount-helper function to change the options of a
single mountpoint. The fragment is sourced before the mountpoints are remounted,
so the redefinition replaces the default. One function per mountpoint:

  _boot _boot_efi _run _dev _dev_shm _proc _sys _tmp _var_tmp _var_log
  _var_cache _var _usr _home _root _srv _media _mnt _opt _etc

Each sets 'mount_folder' and 'intended_mount_options', then calls remount_secure.

Example - also apply noexec to /opt (the default allows exec there):

  sudo tee /etc/remount-secure.d/60_opt-noexec.conf >/dev/null <<'EOF'
  _opt() {
    mount_folder="/opt"
    intended_mount_options="nosuid,nodev,noexec"
    remount_secure
  }
  EOF

Applied on the next boot, or immediately with 'sudo remount-secure --force'
(no level -> resolves the configured level and sources the overrides).

NOTE: fragments are sourced AS ROOT at boot, so only root may write the drop-in
directories (enforced: ownership and mode are checked before sourcing). A broken
override can break boot-time remounting - keep it to the short form above.

Debugging: add 'noremountsecure' to the kernel command line to disable
remount-secure for a single boot without changing the configured level.

